#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FIFTH STAGE

Now, as Christian went on his way, he came to a little rise, which was cast up on purpose so that pilgrims might see ahead of them: up there, therefore, Christian went; and looking forward, he saw Faithful ahead of him on his journey. Then Christian called aloud, Ho, ho; so-ho; wait, and I will be your companion. At that Faithful looked behind him; and Christian cried to him again, Wait, wait, till I come up to you. But Faithful answered, No, I am running for my life, and the avenger of blood is behind me.

At this Christian was somewhat stirred, and putting out all his strength, he quickly caught up with Faithful, and also ran past him; so the last was first. Then Christian smiled vaingloriously, because he had got ahead of his brother; but not taking good heed to his feet, he suddenly stumbled and fell, and could not rise again until Faithful came up to help him.

Then I saw in my dream, they went on together very lovingly, and had sweet talk of all the things that had happened to them in their pilgrimage; and this is how Christian began.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: My honored and well-beloved brother Faithful, I am glad that I have overtaken you, and that God has so tempered our spirits that we can walk as companions in so pleasant a path.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I had thought, my dear friend, to have had your company all the way from our town, but you got the start of me; so I was forced to come this much of the way alone.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How long did you stay in the city of Destruction before you set out after me on your pilgrimage?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Till I could stay no longer; for soon after you had gone out there was a great deal of talk that our city would in a short time be burned down to the ground with fire from heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What, did your neighbors talk like that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, for a while it was in everybody’s mouth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What, and did no more of them but you come out to escape the danger?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Though there was, as I said, a great deal of talk about it, yet I do not think they firmly believed it; for in the heat of the talk I heard some of them speak mockingly of you and of your desperate journey, for that is what they called this pilgrimage of yours. But I believed, and still do, that the end of our city will be with fire and brimstone from above; and therefore I have made my escape.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you hear no talk of neighbor Pliable?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, Christian, I heard that he followed you till he came to the Slough of Despond, where, as some said, he fell in; but he would not be known to have done so: but I am sure he was soundly spattered with that kind of dirt.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did the neighbors say to him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Since he went back he has been greatly mocked, and that among all sorts of people: some mock and despise him, and hardly anyone will give him work. He is now seven times worse than if he had never gone out of the city.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But why should they be so set against him, since they also despise the way that he forsook?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: O, they say, Hang him; he is a turncoat; he was not true to his profession of faith! I think God has stirred up even His enemies to hiss at him, and make him a byword, because he has forsaken the way. Jer 29:18, 19.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you have no talk with him before you came out?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I met him once in the streets, but he slunk away on the other side, as one ashamed of what he had done; so I did not speak to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, at my first setting out I had hopes of that man; but now I fear he will perish in the overthrow of the city. For it has happened to him according to the true proverb, The dog has turned to his own vomit again, and the sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire. 2 Pet 2:22.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: These are my fears for him too; but who can hinder what will be?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, neighbor Faithful, said Christian, let us leave him, and talk of things that concern ourselves more immediately. Tell me now what you have met with on the way as you came; for I know you have met with some things, or else it may be written down for a wonder.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I escaped the slough that I perceive you fell into, and got up to the gate without that danger; only I met with one whose name was Wanton, who came close to doing me harm.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It was well you escaped her net: Joseph was hard put to it by her, and he escaped her as you did; but it nearly cost him his life. Gen 39:11–13. But what did she do to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: You cannot think (unless you know something of it) what a flattering tongue she had; she pressed me hard to turn aside with her, promising me all manner of contentment.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No, she did not promise you the contentment of a good conscience.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: You know what I mean; all carnal and fleshly contentment.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Thank God that you escaped her: the abhorred of the Lord shall fall into her pit. Prov 22:14.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Indeed, I do not know whether I wholly escaped her or not.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, I suppose you did not consent to her desires?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, not to defile myself; for I remembered an old writing that I had seen, which said, “Her steps lead straight to Hell.” Prov 5:5. So I shut my eyes, because I would not be bewitched by her looks. Job 31:1. Then she railed at me, and I went my way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you meet with no other assault as you came?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: When I came to the foot of the hill called Difficulty, I met with a very aged man, who asked me what I was, and where I was bound. I told him that I was a pilgrim, going to the Celestial City. Then the old man said, You look like an honest fellow; will you be content to live with me for the wages that I shall give you? Then I asked his name, and where he lived. He said his name was Adam the First, and that he lived in the town of Deceit. Eph 4:22. I asked him then what his work was, and what wages he would give. He told me that his work was #emph[many delights;] and his wages, that I should be his heir at last. I further asked him what house he kept, and what other servants he had. So he told me that his house was supplied with all the dainties of the world, and that his servants were those of his own begetting. Then I asked how many children he had. He said that he had only three daughters, the Lust of the Flesh, the Lust of the Eyes, and the Pride of Life, 1 John 2:16; and that I should marry them if I would. Then I asked how long he would have me live with him. And he told me, as long as he lived himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and what conclusion did the old man and you come to at last?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Why, at first I found myself somewhat inclined to go with the man, for I thought he spoke very fair; but looking at his forehead as I talked with him, I saw written there, “Put off the old man with his deeds.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Then it came burning hot into my mind that, whatever he said, and however he flattered, when he got me home to his house he would sell me for a slave. So I told him to stop talking, for I would not come near the door of his house. Then he reviled me, and told me that he would send someone after me who would make my way bitter to my soul. So I turned to go away from him; but just as I turned myself to go from there, I felt him take hold of my flesh, and give me such a deadly jerk back that I thought he had pulled part of me after him: this made me cry, “O wretched man.” Rom 7:24. So I went on my way up the hill.]

Now, when I had got more than halfway up, I looked behind me, and saw one coming after me, swift as the wind; so he overtook me just about the place where the settle stands.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Just there, said Christian, I sat down to rest; but being overcome with sleep, I lost this roll out of my bosom there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But, good brother, hear me out. As soon as the man overtook me, it was but a word and a blow; for down he knocked me, and laid me for dead. But when I had come to myself a little again, I asked him why he treated me so. He said it was because of my secret inclining to Adam the First. And with that he struck me another deadly blow on the breast, and beat me down backward; so I lay at his foot as dead as before. So when I came to myself again I begged him for mercy: but he said, I do not know how to show mercy; and with that he knocked me down again. He would doubtless have made an end of me, but that one came by and told him to stop.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Who was it that told him to stop?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I did not know him at first: but as he went by, I saw the holes in his hands and in his side: then I concluded that he was our Lord. So I went up the hill.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That man who overtook you was Moses. He spares none; nor does he know how to show mercy to those who transgress the law.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I know it very well; it was not the first time that he has met with me. It was he who came to me when I lived complacently at home, and who told me he would burn my house over my head if I stayed there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But did you not see the house that stood there on the top of the hill, on the side of which Moses met you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, and the lions too, before I came to it. But as for the lions, I think they were asleep, for it was about noon; and because I had so much of the day before me, I passed by the Porter, and came down the hill.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He told me, indeed, that he saw you go by; but I wish you had called at the house, for they would have shown you so many rarities that you would hardly have forgotten them to the day of your death. But please tell me, did you meet nobody in the Valley of Humility?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, I met with one Discontent, who would gladly have persuaded me to go back again with him: his reason was that the valley was altogether without honor. He told me, moreover, that to go there was the way to lose the goodwill of all my friends, such as Pride, Arrogancy, Self-Conceit, Worldly Glory, and others, who he knew, as he said, would be very much offended if I made such a fool of myself as to wade through this valley.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and how did you answer him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I told him that although all these that he named might claim kinship with me, and that rightly (for indeed they were my relations according to the flesh), yet since I became a pilgrim they have disowned me, and I also have rejected them; and therefore they were now no more to me than if they had never been of my lineage. I told him, moreover, that as to this valley, he had quite misrepresented the thing; for before honor is humility, and a haughty spirit before a fall. Therefore, said I, I would rather go through this valley to the honor that was so accounted by the wisest, than choose that which he esteemed most worthy of our affections.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you meet with nothing else in that valley?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, I met with Shame; but of all the men that I met with on my pilgrimage, he, I think, bears the wrong name. The others would take no for an answer, after a little argument and something else; but this bold-faced Shame would never be done.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what did he say to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: What? Why, he objected against religion itself. He said it was a pitiful, low, sneaking business for a man to mind religion. He said that a tender conscience was an unmanly thing; and that for a man to watch over his words and ways, so as to tie himself up from that hectoring liberty that the brave spirits of the times accustomed themselves to, would make him the ridicule of the times. He objected also that only a few of the mighty, rich, or wise were ever of my opinion; nor any of them either, before they were persuaded to be fools, and of a willing folly to venture the loss of all for nobody knows what. 1 Cor 1:26; 3:18; Phil 3:7–9; John 7:48. He, moreover, objected the base and low estate and condition of those who were chiefly the pilgrims of the times in which they lived; also their ignorance and lack of understanding in all natural science. Yes, he held me to it at that rate also, about a great many more things than I relate here: as, that it was a shame to sit whining and mourning under a sermon, and a shame to come sighing and groaning home; that it was a shame to ask my neighbor’s forgiveness for petty faults, or to make restitution where I have taken from anyone. He said also that religion made a man grow strange to the great, because of a few vices, which he called by finer names, and made him acknowledge and respect the base, because they were of the same religious brotherhood: And is not this, said he, a shame?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you say to him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Say? I could not tell what to say at first. Yes, he put me so to it that my blood came up in my face; even this Shame fetched it up, and had almost beaten me off altogether. But at last I began to consider that that which is highly esteemed among men is held in abomination with God. Luke 16:15. And I thought again, this Shame tells me what men are; but he tells me nothing of what God, or the word of God, is. And I thought, moreover, that at the day of doom we shall not be doomed to death or life according to the hectoring spirits of the world, but according to the wisdom and law of the Highest. Therefore, thought I, what God says is best is indeed best, though all the men in the world are against it. Seeing, then, that God prefers his religion; seeing God prefers a tender conscience; seeing they that make themselves fools for the kingdom of heaven are wisest, and that the poor man who loves Christ is richer than the greatest man in the world who hates him; Shame, depart, you are an enemy to my salvation. Shall I welcome you against my sovereign Lord? How then shall I look him in the face at his coming? Mark 8:38. Should I now be ashamed of his ways and servants, how can I expect the blessing? But indeed this Shame was a bold villain; I could scarcely shake him out of my company; yes, he kept haunting me, and continually whispering in my ear with some one or other of the infirmities that attend religion. But at last I told him that it was but in vain to attempt anything further in this business; for those things that he disdained, in those I saw the most glory: and so at last I got past this importunate one. And when I had shaken him off, then I began to sing,]

#quote[“The trials that those men do meet withal, \
That are obedient to the heavenly call, \
Are manifold, and suited to the flesh, \
And come, and come, and come again afresh; \
That now, or some time else, we by them may \
Be taken, overcome, and cast away. \
O let the pilgrims, let the pilgrims then, \
Be vigilant, and quit themselves like men.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I am glad, my brother, that you withstood this villain so bravely; for of all, as you say, I think he has the wrong name; for he is so bold as to follow us in the streets, and to attempt to put us to shame before all men; that is, to make us ashamed of that which is good. But if he were not himself audacious, he would never attempt to do as he does. But let us still resist him; for, notwithstanding all his bravado, he promotes the fool, and no one else. “The wise will inherit glory,” said Solomon, “but shame will be the promotion of fools.” Prov 3:35.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I think we must cry for help against Shame to Him who would have us be valiant for truth upon the earth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You speak truly; but did you meet nobody else in that valley?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, not I; for I had sunshine all the rest of the way through that, and also through the Valley of the Shadow of Death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It was well for you; I am sure it fared far otherwise with me. For a long time, almost as soon as I entered that valley, I had a dreadful combat with that foul fiend Apollyon; yes, I truly thought he would have killed me, especially when he got me down and crushed me under him, as if he would have crushed me to pieces; for as he threw me, my sword flew out of my hand: indeed, he told me he was sure of me; but I cried to God, and he heard me, and delivered me out of all my troubles. Then I entered into the Valley of the Shadow of Death, and had no light for almost half the way through it. I thought I would be killed there over and over; but at last day broke, and the sun rose, and I went through what remained with far more ease and quiet.]

Moreover, I saw in my dream that as they went on, Faithful, as he chanced to look to one side, saw a man whose name was Talkative, walking at a distance beside them; for in this place there was room enough for them all to walk. He was a tall man, and somewhat better-looking at a distance than close at hand. To this man Faithful addressed himself in this manner.

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Friend, where are you going? Are you going to the heavenly country?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I am going to the same place.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is good; then I hope we shall have your good company?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: With a very good will I will be your companion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Come on, then, and let us go together, and let us spend our time in talking of things that are profitable.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: To talk of things that are good is very acceptable to me, with you or with any other; and I am glad that I have met with those who incline to so good a work; for, to speak the truth, there are only a few who care to spend their time this way as they travel, but much rather choose to be speaking of things to no profit; and this has been a trouble to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is indeed a thing to be lamented; for what thing is so worthy of the use of the tongue and mouth of men on earth as the things of the God of heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I like you wonderfully well, for your saying is full of conviction; and I will add, What thing is so pleasant, and what so profitable, as to talk of the things of God? What things so pleasant? That is, if a man has any delight in things that are wonderful. For instance, if a man delights to talk of the history or the mystery of things; or if a man loves to talk of miracles, wonders, or signs, where will he find things recorded so delightful, and so sweetly penned, as in the holy Scripture?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is true; but to be profited by such things in our talk should be our chief design.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: That is what I said; for to talk of such things is most profitable; for by so doing a man may get knowledge of many things; as of the vanity of earthly things, and the benefit of things above. Thus in general; but more particularly, by this a man may learn the necessity of the new birth, the insufficiency of our works, the need of Christ’s righteousness, etc. Besides, by this a man may learn what it is to repent, to believe, to pray, to suffer, or the like: by this, also, a man may learn what are the great promises and consolations of the Gospel, to his own comfort. Further, by this a man may learn to refute false opinions, to vindicate the truth, and also to instruct the ignorant.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: All this is true; and I am glad to hear these things from you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Alas! the lack of this is the reason that so few understand the need of faith, and the necessity of a work of grace in their soul, for eternal life; but ignorantly live in the works of the law, by which a man can by no means obtain the kingdom of heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But, by your leave, heavenly knowledge of these is the gift of God; no man attains to them by human effort, or only by talking of them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: All this I know very well; for a man can receive nothing unless it is given him from heaven: all is of grace, not of works. I could give you a hundred scriptures to confirm this.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, then, said Faithful, what is that one thing that we shall at this time found our talk upon?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: What you will. I will talk of things heavenly, or things earthly; things moral, or things evangelical; things sacred, or things profane; things past, or things to come; things foreign, or things at home; things more essential, or things circumstantial: provided that all is done to our profit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Now Faithful began to wonder; and stepping to Christian (for he walked all this while by himself), he said to him, but softly, What a splendid companion we have got! Surely this man will make a very excellent pilgrim.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: At this Christian modestly smiled, and said, This man, with whom you are so taken, will beguile with this tongue of his twenty of those who do not know him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Do you know him, then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Know him? Yes, better than he knows himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Tell me, what is he?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: His name is Talkative: he lives in our town. I wonder that you should be a stranger to him, except that I consider that our town is large.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Whose son is he? And whereabouts does he live?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He is the son of one Say-well. He lived in Prating-Row; and he is known to all who are acquainted with him by the name of Talkative of Prating-Row; and, notwithstanding his fine tongue, he is but a sorry fellow.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, he seems to be a very fine man.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That is, to those who do not have a thorough acquaintance with him, for he is best away from home; near home he is ugly enough. Your saying that he is a fine man brings to my mind what I have observed in the work of a painter, whose pictures show best at a distance, but very near are more unpleasing.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But I am ready to think you are only joking, because you smiled.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: God forbid that I should joke (though I smiled) in this matter, or that I should accuse anyone falsely. I will show you more of him. This man is for any company, and for any talk; as he talks now with you, so will he talk when he is on the ale-bench; and the more drink he has in his head, the more of these things he has in his mouth. Religion has no place in his heart, or house, or life; all he has lies in his tongue, and his religion is to make a noise with it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Do you say so? Then I am greatly deceived in this man.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Deceived! You may be sure of it. Remember the proverb, “They say, and do not”; but the kingdom of God is not in word, but in power. Matt 23:3; 1 Cor 4:20. He talks of prayer, of repentance, of faith, and of the new birth; but he knows only how to talk of them. I have been in his family, and have observed him both at home and away from home; and I know that what I say of him is the truth. His house is as empty of religion as the white of an egg is of savor. There is neither prayer there, nor sign of repentance for sin; yes, the brute, in its own way, serves God far better than he. He is the very stain, reproach, and shame of religion to all who know him, Rom 2:24, 25; through him, religion can hardly have a good word in all that end of the town where he lives. So say the common people who know him, “A saint abroad, and a devil at home.” His poor family finds it so; he is such a churl, such a railer at his servants, and so unreasonable with them, that they neither know how to do for him nor how to speak to him. Men who have any dealings with him say, It is better to deal with a Turk than with him, for they will have fairer dealings at their hands. This Talkative (if it is possible) will go beyond them, defraud, beguile, and overreach them. Besides, he brings up his sons to follow in his steps; and if he finds in any of them a foolish timidity (for so he calls the first appearance of a tender conscience), he calls them fools and blockheads, and will by no means employ them in much, or speak in their praise before others. For my part, I am of the opinion that he has, by his wicked life, caused many to stumble and fall; and will be, unless God prevents it, the ruin of many more.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, my brother, I am bound to believe you, not only because you say you know him, but also because you make your reports of men like a Christian. For I cannot think that you speak these things out of ill will, but because it is even so as you say.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Had I known him no more than you, I might perhaps have thought of him as you did at first; yes, had I received this report only at the hands of those who are enemies to religion, I would have thought it was a slander—a lot that often falls from bad men’s mouths upon good men’s names and professions of faith. But all these things, yes, and a great many more as bad, I can prove him guilty of from my own knowledge. Besides, good men are ashamed of him; they can call him neither brother nor friend; the very naming of him among them makes them blush, if they know him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, I see that saying and doing are two things, and hereafter I shall better observe this distinction.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: They are two things indeed, and are as different as the soul and the body; for as the body without the soul is but a dead carcass, so #emph[saying], if it is alone, is but a dead carcass also. The soul of religion is the practical part. “Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress, and to keep oneself from being polluted by the world.” Jas 1:27; see also verses 22-26. This Talkative is not aware of; he thinks that hearing and saying will make a good Christian; and so he deceives his own soul. Hearing is but as the sowing of the seed; talking is not sufficient to prove that fruit is indeed in the heart and life. And let us assure ourselves that at the day of doom men shall be judged according to their fruits. Matt 13:23. It will not be said then, Did you believe? but, Were you doers, or talkers only? and they shall be judged accordingly. The end of the world is compared to our harvest, Matt 13:30, and you know men at harvest regard nothing but fruit. Not that anything can be accepted that is not of faith; but I speak this to show you how insignificant Talkative’s profession of faith will be at that day.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: This brings to my mind that word of Moses by which he describes the beast that is clean. Lev 11; Deut 14. It is one that parts the hoof and chews the cud; not one that parts the hoof only, or that chews the cud only. The hare chews the cud, but yet is unclean, because he does not part the hoof. And this truly resembles Talkative: he chews the cud, he seeks knowledge; he chews upon the word, but he does not divide the hoof. He does not part with the way of sinners; but, like the hare, he keeps the foot of the dog or bear, and therefore he is unclean.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You have spoken, as far as I know, the true gospel sense of these texts. And I will add another thing: Paul calls some men, yes, and those great talkers too, sounding brass and tinkling cymbals, 1 Cor 13:1, 3; that is, as he explains them in another place, things without life giving sound. 1 Cor 14:7. Things without life; that is, without the true faith and grace of the gospel; and consequently, things that shall never be placed in the kingdom of heaven among those who are the children of life; though their sound, by their talk, is as if it were the tongue or voice of an angel.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, I was not so fond of his company at first as I am sick of it now. What shall we do to be rid of him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Take my advice, and do as I tell you, and you will find that he will soon be sick of your company too, unless God touches his heart and turns it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: What would you have me do?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, go to him, and enter into some serious talk about the power of religion; and ask him plainly (when he has approved of it, for that he will) whether this thing is set up in his heart, house, or life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Then Faithful stepped forward again, and said to Talkative, Come, what cheer? How is it now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Well, thank you: I thought we would have had a great deal of talk by this time.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you will, we will fall to it now; and since you left it to me to state the question, let it be this: How does the saving grace of God show itself when it is in the heart of man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I perceive, then, that our talk must be about the power of things. Well, it is a very good question, and I shall be willing to answer you. And take my answer in brief, thus: First, where the grace of God is in the heart, it causes a great outcry there against sin. Secondly—]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, hold on; let us consider one at a time. I think you should rather say, it shows itself by inclining the soul to abhor its sin.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Why, what difference is there between crying out against sin and abhorring it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Oh! a great deal. A man may cry out against sin out of self-interest; but he cannot abhor it except by virtue of a godly antipathy against it. I have heard many cry out against sin in the pulpit, who yet can tolerate it well enough in the heart, house, and life. Gen 39:15. Joseph’s mistress cried out with a loud voice, as if she had been very holy; but she would willingly, notwithstanding that, have committed uncleanness with him. Some cry out against sin even as the mother cries out against her child in her lap, when she calls it little rascal and naughty girl, and then falls to hugging and kissing it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You are lying in wait to catch me, I perceive.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, not I; I am only for setting things right. But what is the second thing by which you would prove that a work of grace is shown in the heart?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Great knowledge of gospel mysteries.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: This sign should have been first: but, first or last, it is also false; for knowledge, great knowledge, may be obtained in the mysteries of the Gospel, and yet there be no work of grace in the soul. Yes, if a man has all knowledge, he may yet be nothing, and so, consequently, be no child of God. 1 Cor 13:2. When Christ said, “Do you#super[+] know all these things?” and the disciples answered, Yes, he added, “You will be blessed if you do them.” He does not lay the blessing in the knowing of them, but in the doing of them. For there is a knowledge that is not attended with doing: “He who knows his Master’s will, and does not do it.” A man may know like an angel, and yet be no Christian: therefore your sign of it is not true. Indeed, to #emph[know] is a thing that pleases talkers and boasters; but to #emph[do] is that which pleases God. Not that the heart can be good without knowledge, for without that the heart is worthless. There are, therefore, two sorts of knowledge: knowledge that rests in the bare speculation of things, and knowledge that is accompanied with the grace of faith and love, which sets a man to doing even the will of God from the heart. The first of these will serve the talker; but without the other, the true Christian is not content. “Give me understanding that I may obey Your law, and follow it with all my heart.” Ps 119:34.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You are lying in wait to catch me again: this is not for edification.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you please, propose another sign of how this work of grace shows itself where it is.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Not I, for I see we shall not agree.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you will not, will you give me leave to do it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You may use your liberty.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: A work of grace in the soul shows itself either to him who has it, or to those standing by.]

To him who has it, thus: It gives him conviction of sin, especially of the defilement of his nature, and the sin of unbelief, for the sake of which he is sure to be damned if he does not find mercy at God’s hand by faith in Jesus Christ. This sight and sense of things works in him sorrow and shame for sin. Ps 38:18; Jer 31:19; John 16:8; Rom 7:24; Mark 16:16; Gal 2:16; Rev 1:6. He finds, moreover, revealed in him the Savior of the world, and the absolute necessity of embracing him for life; at which he finds hungerings and thirstings after him; to which hungerings, etc., the promise is made. Now, according to the strength or weakness of his faith in his Savior, so is his joy and peace, so is his love of holiness, so are his desires to know him more, and also to serve him in this world. But though, I say, it shows itself thus to him, yet it is only seldom that he is able to conclude that this is a work of grace; because his corruptions now, and his abused reason, make his mind misjudge in this matter: therefore in him who has this work there is required a very sound judgment before he can conclude with steadiness that this is a work of grace. John 16:9; Gal 2:15, 16; Acts 4:12; Matt 5:6; Rev 21:6.

To others it is shown thus:

1\. By a confession of his faith in Christ from experience. 2. By a life matching that confession; that is, a life of holiness—heart-holiness, family-holiness (if he has a family), and holiness of conduct in the world; which in general teaches him inwardly to abhor his sin, and himself for it, in secret; to suppress it in his family, and to promote holiness in the world: not by talk only, as a hypocrite or talkative person may do, but by a practical subjection in faith and love to the power of the word. Job 42:5, 6; Ps 50:23; Ezek 20:43; Matt 5:8; John 14:15; Rom 10:10; Ezek 36:25; Phil 1:27; 3:17–20. And now, sir, as to this brief description of the work of grace, and also of the way it is shown, if you have anything to object, object; if not, then give me leave to put a second question to you.

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: No, my part now is not to object, but to hear; let me, therefore, have your second question.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: It is this: Do you experience this first part of the description of it; and do your life and conduct testify the same? Or does your religion stand in word or tongue, and not in deed and truth? Please, if you are inclined to answer me in this, say no more than you know the God above will say Amen to, and also nothing but what your conscience can justify you in; for it is not the one who commends himself who is approved, but the one whom the Lord commends. Besides, to say I am thus and thus, when my conduct, and all my neighbors, tell me I lie, is great wickedness.]

Then Talkative at first began to blush; but, recovering himself, he replied thus: You come now to experience, to conscience, and to God; and to appeal to him for justification of what is spoken. This kind of talk I did not expect; nor am I disposed to give an answer to such questions, because I do not count myself bound to it, unless you take upon yourself to be a catechist; and even if you should do so, yet I may refuse to make you my judge. But please, will you tell me why you ask me such questions?

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Because I saw you eager to talk, and because I did not know that you had anything else but notions. Besides, to tell you all the truth, I have heard of you that you are a man whose religion lies in talk, and that your conduct gives the lie to this profession of your mouth. They say you are a spot among Christians, and that religion fares the worse for your ungodly conduct; that some have already stumbled at your wicked ways, and that more are in danger of being destroyed by them: your religion, and an ale-house, and covetousness, and uncleanness, and swearing, and lying, and vain company-keeping, etc., will stand together. The proverb is true of you which is said of a harlot, that is, “That she is a shame to all women”: so you are a shame to all professing Christians.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Since you are so ready to take up reports, and to judge as rashly as you do, I cannot but conclude that you are some peevish or melancholy man, not fit to be talked with; and so adieu.]

Then up came Christian, and said to his brother, I told you how it would happen; your words and his lusts could not agree. He would rather leave your company than reform his life. But he is gone, as I said: let him go; the loss is no man’s but his own. He has saved us the trouble of going from him; for if he continued (as I suppose he will) as he is, he would have been but a blot in our company: besides, the apostle says, “From such withdraw yourself.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But I am glad we had this little talk with him; it may happen that he will think of it again: however, I have dealt plainly with him, and so am clear of his blood if he perishes.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You did well to talk so plainly to him as you did. There is but little of this faithful dealing with men nowadays, and that makes religion stink so in the nostrils of many as it does; for they are these talkative fools, whose religion is only in word, and who are debauched and vain in their conduct, that (being so much admitted into the fellowship of the godly) puzzle the world, blemish Christianity, and grieve the sincere. I wish that all men would deal with such as you have done; then they would either be made more conformable to religion, or the company of saints would be too hot for them. Then Faithful said,]

#quote[“How Talkative at first lifts up his plumes! \
How bravely doth he speak! How he presumes \
To drive down all before him! But so soon \
As Faithful talks of heart-work, like the moon \
That’s past the full, into the wane he goes; \
And so will all but he that heart-work know.”]

Thus they went on, talking of what they had seen by the way, and so made that way easy which would otherwise no doubt have been tedious to them, for now they went through a wilderness.
